10.3 Shell
==========

‘run_shell’ runs one command in the project root through your shell.
The next command starts in the same directory when a ‘cd’ stayed inside
the project.  Nil and ‘""’ mean the project root.  A ‘cd’ that leaves
the project is not kept.  Harmless reports ‘The shell left the project.
The next command starts in ’, followed by the directory it kept.  The
filesystem root is not kept.  The remembered directory is saved with the
session.

   ‘timeout’ is how many seconds to wait.  When it is omitted,
‘harmless-shell-timeout-seconds’ (120) is used.  A negative number,
zero, or a value that is not a number is refused: ‘Error: timeout must
be a positive number’.  An empty command is refused: ‘Error: command is
empty’.  When the command is still running at the end of that wait,
Harmless leaves the process running and returns a background id with the
output captured so far.  It does not kill the process.  ‘background’
true returns that id immediately.

   ‘shell_output’ reads one background shell.  ‘id’ is the id
‘run_shell’ returned.  Without ‘timeout’, the tool returns the output
already available.  With ‘timeout’, it waits that many seconds for the
command to exit, then returns whatever it has.  This tool does not ask
for permission.  An empty id is refused: ‘Error: shell id is empty’.  An
unknown id is refused: ‘Error: no background shell ’, followed by that
id.  A bad timeout uses the same refusal as ‘run_shell’.

   ‘shell_kill’ stops a background shell.  It asks for permission like
any other shell command.  Killing a shell that is not running is
refused: ‘Error: shell ’, the id, and ‘ is not running’.

   The text returned to the model is cut at
‘harmless-shell-output-max-bytes’ (20000).

   Abort stops a shell that is still in the foreground.  A shell that
has moved to the background keeps running.  When it exits, or when it is
killed, Emacs shows a message and a note in the transcript.  That note
is not a message the model sees on the next turn.  The jobs are kept in
memory for this Emacs session.  They are not written to the session
files.

   A command Harmless recognizes as read-only does not ask for
permission.  A command it does not recognize still asks.  A redirect
that writes, a command substitution, and a subshell ask.  *Note
Permissions::.

   In plan mode, a shell command that can write is refused before any
permission question: ‘Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can
write is refused’.  Nil, ‘""’, and a blank command are not that refusal.
A read-only command still runs.  ‘shell_kill’ still asks.  *Note Plan
mode::.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Questions,  Next: Subagents,  Prev: Shell,  Up: Tools

10.4 Questions
==============

‘ask_user’ asks one question.  ‘question’ is the prompt.  ‘choices’ are
the answers.  Emacs asks with completion, and the answer must be one of
those choices.  The tool returns ‘The user chose: ’ followed by that
answer.

   A missing or blank question is refused.  A missing choice list, an
empty list, a blank choice, a choice that spans lines, and a repeated
choice are refused.  A choice is text.  A value such as ‘/’ or ‘lisp/’
is returned as that text.  Cancelling the prompt returns ‘The user
cancelled the question.’

   The tool does not ask for permission first.  A detached chat can use
it.  Plan mode can use it.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Subagents,  Next: Workflows,  Prev: Questions,  Up: Tools

10.5 Subagents
==============

‘spawn_agent’ starts a child session and waits for its last reply.  The
child has its own transcript.  It does not see the parent's messages.
‘prompt’ is the task.  Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘prompt is
empty’.  ‘description’ is an optional one-line title.  A value such as
‘/’ or ‘sub/’ is a title, not a path.  ‘worktree’ is an optional
checkout name.  The parent project stays where it is.  The child edits
that checkout.  *Note Worktrees::.

   ‘agent’ is an optional definition name.  It is not a path.  ‘""’  is
refused: ‘agent name is empty’.  ‘sub/’, ‘/’, and ‘c:/’ are refused:
‘agent name is not a name’.  A name with no definition is refused: ‘no
agent named ’, followed by that name.  ‘sub’ is a name.  With no file it
is ‘no agent named sub’.

   Harmless reads ‘NAME.md’ from these directories, at every level from
your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem root.
A definition closer to the session replaces one of the same name farther
away.  In one directory, later entries in this list replace earlier
ones:

  1. ‘.agents/agents/’

  2. ‘.codex/agents/’

  3. ‘.claude/agents/’

  4. ‘.grok/agents/’

  5. ‘.harmless/agents/’

   A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter:

     ---
     name: reviewer
     description: Reviews code
     tools: read_file, grep
     model: grok-4.6
     sandbox: read-only
     ---
     Report findings.  Do not edit files.

   ‘tools’ limits the child.  An unknown tool name is refused.
‘spawn_agent’ cannot be one of them.  ‘model’ replaces the parent's
model.  ‘inherit’ keeps it.  ‘sandbox’ is ‘workspace’ or ‘read-only’.
Anything else is refused: ‘sandbox must be workspace or read-only’.  The
body is extra instructions for that child.  Each turn lists the
available agents for the parent.  A subagent does not receive that list.

   A read-only child may read files and run a shell command that cannot
write.  A tool that can write is refused before the permission question:
‘This subagent is read-only. A tool that can write is refused’.  A blank
shell command is not that refusal.  A read-only child may send a finding
to another live session.  *Note Messages::.  It may also search the web.
*Note Web search::.  It may fetch a page.  *Note Fetch a page::.  It may
search X. *Note X search::.  It may list and read MCP resources.  *Note
MCP::.  A read-only child is not offered computer control.  *Note
Computer use::.  It may watch a file or a read-only command.  A command
that can write is refused.  *Note Monitors::.  A workspace child uses
the parent's permission mode.

   In plan mode, a subagent that can write is refused before the
permission question, including when the mode is ‘always-approve’: ‘Plan
mode is on. A subagent that can write is refused’.  A read-only subagent
still runs.  A worktree is refused even for a read-only child: ‘Plan
mode is on. A worktree is refused’.

   The child cannot start another subagent: ‘a subagent cannot start
another subagent’.  A tool outside its list is refused: ‘this subagent
cannot use ’, followed by the tool name.

   ‘harmless-agent-max-turns’ (8) is how many provider calls the child
may make.  The next call stops it: ‘subagent stopped after ’, the
number, and ‘ turns’.  The value must be a positive integer.

   Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The parent
does not record a summary for that call.  Aborting only the child
returns ‘subagent aborted’.  An empty child reply is ‘The model returned
an empty reply.’

   The child's sandbox, tool list, and extra prompt are saved with the
session.  The running callback is not.  A detached chat can start a
child with no definition.  A named definition needs a project directory.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Workflows,  Next: Worktrees,  Prev: Subagents,  Up: Tools

10.6 Workflows
==============

‘run_workflow’ runs one script.  The script starts child sessions.  The
tool returns one string, the value of ‘complete’.  The parent transcript
does not gain a message for each child.  Pass ‘name’ or ‘script’, not
both.  Neither is refused: ‘workflow needs a name or a script’.  Both
are refused: ‘workflow takes a name or a script’.

   ‘script’ is the text of a script written for this task.  A value that
is not text is refused: ‘workflow script must be a string’.  ‘""’  and
whitespace are refused: ‘workflow script is empty’.  The script is not
Emacs Lisp.  Harmless does not evaluate it.  A form it does not know is
refused: ‘unknown workflow expression’.  Text the reader cannot read is
refused: ‘workflow script is invalid’.

   ‘name’ is a fixed script, not a path.  ‘""’  is refused: ‘workflow
name is empty’.  ‘sub/’, ‘/’, and ‘c:/’ are refused: ‘workflow name is
not a name’.  A name with no file is refused: ‘no workflow named ’,
followed by that name.  ‘sub’ is a name.  With no file it is ‘no
workflow named sub’.  ‘description’ is an optional one-line title.  A
value such as ‘/’ or ‘sub/’ is a title, not a path.

   Harmless reads ‘NAME.hw’ from these directories, at every level from
your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem root.
A script closer to the session replaces one of the same name farther
away.  In one directory, later entries in this list replace earlier
ones:

  1. ‘.agents/workflows/’

  2. ‘.codex/workflows/’

  3. ‘.claude/workflows/’

  4. ‘.grok/workflows/’

  5. ‘.harmless/workflows/’

   A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter.  The body is the script:

     ---
     name: review
     description: Review the diff
     ---
     (let security (agent "Review security" :sandbox read-only))
     (let tests (agent "Review tests" :sandbox read-only))
     (complete (join "\n" security tests))

   ‘name’ in the frontmatter is the script name.  When it is empty, the
file name is the name.  A file that does not parse is left out of the
list.  Asking for it still reports the error.

   The forms are ‘let’ and ‘complete’.  ‘complete’ is required and must
be last.  A script with none is refused: ‘workflow has no complete’.  A
form after it is refused: ‘workflow has forms after complete’.  ‘let’
binds a name to an expression.  Binding it again is refused.  A name
such as ‘sub/’ is refused: ‘workflow variable is not a name’.

   An expression is a string, a bound name, ‘agent’, ‘parallel’, or
‘join’.  ‘(agent PROMPT)’ starts one child.  ‘PROMPT’ may be an
expression.  Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘prompt is empty’.
‘:sandbox’ is ‘read-only’ or ‘workspace’.  The default is ‘workspace’.
Anything else is refused: ‘sandbox must be workspace or read-only’.
‘:agent’ is an optional definition name, the same names as
‘spawn_agent’.  ‘:worktree’ is an optional checkout name.  It counts as
a write even when the child is read-only.  *Note Worktrees::.  ‘:label’
and ‘:description’ are optional one-line titles.  ‘(parallel (agent ...)
...)’ starts those children together and keeps their replies in that
order.  An empty list is refused.  ‘(join SEP EXPR ...)’ joins text with
‘SEP’, which must be a string.  A list of replies is joined the same
way.  ‘complete’ of a list joins it with newlines, so the tool still
returns one string.

   ‘harmless-workflow-max-agents’ (8) is how many children one script
may start.  More than that is refused before the first child: ‘workflow
agent budget is ’, followed by the limit.  The value must be a positive
integer.

   A read-only child is one whose ‘:sandbox’ is ‘read-only’, or whose
definition is read-only.  A workflow whose children are all read-only,
and that starts no worktree, does not ask for permission.  A workflow
that can write asks, unless the permission mode already allows edits.
In plan mode, a workflow that can write is refused before the permission
question, including when the mode is ‘always-approve’: ‘Error: Plan mode
is on. A workflow that can write is refused’.  A script that does not
parse is not that refusal.  A read-only workflow still runs.

   A subagent cannot run a workflow: ‘a subagent cannot start a
workflow’.  A definition that lists ‘run_workflow’ is refused the same
way.  The child cannot start a workflow or another subagent.

   Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The
workflow's result is not added.  A detached chat can run an inline
script.  A named script needs a project directory.  Nil, ‘""’, ‘sub’,
‘sub/’, and ‘/’ are not directories Harmless searches.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Worktrees,  Next: Messages,  Prev: Workflows,  Up: Tools

10.7 Worktrees
==============

‘enter_worktree’ points this session at an isolated checkout of the same
repository.  ‘name’ is required.  Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused:
‘worktree name is empty’.  ‘sub/’, ‘/’, and ‘c:/’ are refused: ‘worktree
name is not a name’.  ‘sub’ is a name.  A value such as ‘/’ passed as
‘description’ on another tool is not a worktree name.

   The checkout is created with git, under ‘harmless-directory’, outside
the project.  The branch is ‘harmless/’ plus the name, starting at the
session's current ‘HEAD’.  Files that are not committed are not copied.
The same name is the same checkout.  A second session that enters it
sees the files already there.  Two different names are two checkouts: a
write in one does not change the other, and neither write changes the
original project.

   Harmless will not store a checkout at the filesystem root, at a
relative path, or inside the project.  Nil, ‘""’, ‘sub’, ‘sub/’, and ‘/’
are not a project: ‘This chat is not attached to a project’.  A drive
root is not ‘/’.  A directory that is not a git working tree is refused:
‘this project is not a git repository’.  When git itself cannot be run:
‘git is not available’.  A branch that already exists is refused:
‘worktree branch already exists: harmless/’, followed by the name.  A
file already at the checkout path is refused: ‘worktree path already
exists’.

   Entering sets the session's project to that checkout and clears a
remembered shell directory.  The session files stay in the directory
they already used.  ‘M-x harmless-worktree’ asks for the name.
‘exit_worktree’, or ‘M-x harmless-worktree-exit’, points the session
back at the project it left.  The checkout stays on disk.  A session
that was not entered this way is refused: ‘this session is not in a
worktree’.  A stored return path that is missing, relative, empty, or
‘/’ is refused: ‘worktree original is not a directory’, and the session
stays where it is.

   The tool is an edit.  Ask mode asks before creating the checkout.
Accept-edits and always-approve do not ask.  In plan mode the checkout
is refused before the question, including when the mode is
‘always-approve’: ‘Error: Plan mode is on. A worktree is refused’.
Leaving does not create a checkout, so plan mode allows it.

   ‘spawn_agent’ takes the same ‘worktree’ name.  The child edits the
new checkout.  The parent stays in its project.  A child born in a
checkout has that checkout as its project; it was not moved there, so
leaving says it is not in a worktree.  A read-only child still cannot
write, and it is not offered ‘enter_worktree’.  Creating the checkout is
a write, so plan mode refuses it even for that child.  A workflow agent
accepts ‘:worktree’.  That script can write.  A fork keeps the checkout
the session is in.  It does not create another one, and it can leave.
Aborting a turn does not delete a checkout.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Messages,  Next: Web search,  Prev: Worktrees,  Up: Tools

10.8 Messages
=============

‘list_sessions’ lists the sessions that are live in this Emacs.  Each
line is the id, the status, and the title.  A session that is only on
disk is not listed.  Nil and an empty registry still return the heading
‘Live sessions:’.

   ‘send_message’ sends ‘text’ to the live session whose id is
‘session’.  Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘session id is
empty’.  A value that is not text is refused: ‘session id is not an id’.
A missing or non-text finding is refused: ‘message must be a string’.
‘""’  and whitespace are refused: ‘message is empty’.  The sender is
refused: ‘a session cannot message itself’.  An id that is not live,
including one that was closed, is refused: ‘no live session’ followed by
that id.  ‘sub’, ‘sub/’, ‘/’, and ‘c:/’ are ids, not paths.

   The finding is a line in the other session's transcript.  It does not
start a turn, and it does not change that session's title.  A session
that is idle receives it immediately: ‘Delivered to ’, followed by the
id.  A session that is streaming or waiting on permission keeps it in
memory until the transcript can take a line: ‘Queued for ’, followed by
the id.  That line is written before the next provider call, or when the
turn becomes idle or ends in an error.  Aborting the turn writes a
finding that was still queued.  Closing Emacs before that write drops
it.  The finding is not a task, and it is not kept for a session that
later replaces this one.

   The tool asks in ‘ask’ mode.  ‘accept-edits’ allows it.  Plan mode
does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may send one.  A detached chat
may list sessions and send a finding.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Web search,  Next: Fetch a page,  Prev: Messages,  Up: Tools

10.9 Web search
===============

‘web_search’ looks up ‘query’ on the web.  The query is sent to
DuckDuckGo over https.  Nothing is written into the project.  Nil is
refused: ‘query must be a string’.  ‘""’  and whitespace are refused:
‘query is empty’.  A value such as ‘/’ or ‘c:/’ is text to search for,
not a path.

   Each hit is a title, a URL, and a snippet.  No hits returns ‘No
results’.  At most eight hits are returned.  The same URL is listed
once.

   The tool's class is ‘network’.  It asks in ‘ask’ mode and in
‘accept-edits’.  ‘always-approve’ does not ask.  Plan mode does not
refuse it.  A read-only subagent may search.  A detached chat may
search.  A failed request is ‘web search failed’.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Fetch a page,  Next: X search,  Prev: Web search,  Up: Tools

10.10 Fetch a page
==================

‘web_fetch’ reads one page at ‘url’.  Only ‘http’ and ‘https’ are
accepted.  Nothing is written into the project.

   Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘url is empty’.  A value that
is not text is refused: ‘url must be a string’.  A path, another scheme,
or a URL with a space is refused: ‘url must be http or https’.  ‘/’,
‘c:/’, ‘sub’, and ‘file:///’ are not pages.  A scheme without ‘://’ is
refused too.

   HTML is reduced to text.  A script, a style sheet, and a comment are
dropped.  A link keeps its label.  A body that contains an HTML tag is
reduced the same way.  Other text is returned as text.  Surrounding
whitespace is dropped.  An empty page returns ‘Empty page’.  A page
longer than 100000 characters is cut and ends with ‘[truncated]’.

   The tool's class is ‘network’.  It asks in ‘ask’ mode and in
‘accept-edits’.  ‘always-approve’ does not ask.  Plan mode does not
refuse it.  A read-only subagent may fetch a page.  A detached chat may
fetch a page.  A failed request is ‘web request failed’.


File: harmless.info,  Node: X search,  Next: MCP,  Prev: Fetch a page,  Up: Tools

10.11 X search
==============

‘x_keyword_search’ finds recent posts on X. The search covers the last 7
days.  ‘query’ is the text, including search operators.  ‘/’ and ‘c:/’
are text, not paths.  ‘mode’ is ‘Top’ or ‘Latest’.  Latest is the
default.  Top asks X for relevancy.  Latest asks for the newest posts.
‘from_date’ and ‘to_date’ are ‘YYYY-MM-DD’.  ‘to_date’ is the day after
the last day you want.  A date that is not that shape is refused: ‘date
must be YYYY-MM-DD’.  A start after the end is refused: ‘from_date is
after to_date’.

   Nil is refused: ‘query must be a string’.  ‘""’  and whitespace are
refused: ‘query is empty’.  An unknown mode is refused: ‘mode must be
Top or Latest’.  A limit that is not a whole number is refused: ‘limit
must be a positive integer’.  A limit outside 1 to 10 is refused: ‘limit
must be from 1 to 10’.  The default limit is 8.

   Each post is a handle, a name, a time, a URL, and the text.  No posts
returns ‘No posts’.

   ‘x_thread_fetch’ reads ‘post_id’ and the posts around it.  Nil, ‘""’,
and whitespace are refused: ‘post id is empty’.  A value that is not
text is refused: ‘post id must be a string’.  Anything that is not a
decimal id, including ‘/’ and ‘c:/’, is refused: ‘post id is not an id’.
The result is at most 8 posts, oldest first, and it includes the
requested post.  No posts returns ‘No posts’.

   ‘x_user_search’ finds accounts by name, handle, or bio.  The query
and limit refusals match post search.  Each account is a name, a handle,
a URL, and a bio.  No accounts returns ‘No users’.

   The bearer token is ‘harmless-x-bearer’, or the environment variable
‘X_BEARER_TOKEN’, or an auth-source entry for host ‘api.x.com’ and user
‘apikey’.  A missing token is refused before a request: ‘X is not signed
in’.

   The tools' class is ‘network’.  They ask in ‘ask’ mode and in
‘accept-edits’.  ‘always-approve’ does not ask.  Plan mode does not
refuse them.  A read-only subagent may search X. A detached chat may
search X. A response that is not JSON is ‘X search failed’.  A request
that does not succeed is ‘web request failed’.  Nothing is written into
the project.


File: harmless.info,  Node: MCP,  Next: Computer use,  Prev: X search,  Up: Tools

10.12 MCP
=========

Harmless calls tools on a local MCP server and reads that server's
resources.  Servers are named in ‘mcp.json’.  The project file is
‘.harmless/mcp.json’ in the session directory.  The global file is
‘mcp.json’ under the Harmless data directory.  A name in both files uses
the project command.  A detached chat reads only the global file.  A
relative directory, an empty directory, and the filesystem root do not
select a project file.

   The file is a JSON object.  ‘mcpServers’ maps a server name to a
‘command’ and an optional ‘args’ list.  A name that is not a single
token is refused: ‘MCP server name is not a name’.  ‘sub’ with
surrounding spaces is that token.  A missing command is refused: ‘MCP
command is empty’.  A command that is not text is refused: ‘MCP command
must be a string’.  An argument that is not text is refused: ‘MCP
argument must be a string’.  A file that is not JSON is refused:
‘mcp.json is not valid’.  A file with no ‘mcpServers’ object is refused:
‘mcp.json has no servers’.  A missing file means no servers.

   Each server tool is offered as ‘mcp__’, the server name, ‘__’, and
the tool name.  The offered schema is an empty object.  Harmless loads
the server's schema when the tool is about to be called.  A missing
required argument is refused: ‘MCP argument ’, the name, and ‘ is
required’.  The class is ‘network’.  A tool from the server is not
offered to a read-only subagent, because that tool can write.

   ‘mcp_list_resources’ lists resources for ‘server’.
‘mcp_read_resource’ reads ‘uri’ from that server.  Nil, ‘""’, and
whitespace are refused: ‘server name is empty’ or ‘resource uri is
empty’.  A value that is not text is refused: ‘server name must be a
string’ or ‘resource uri must be a string’.  A server name that is not a
token, including ‘/’ and ‘c:/’, is refused: ‘server name is not a name’.
‘/’ and ‘c:/’ are ordinary resource URIs.  An unknown server is refused:
‘no MCP server ’, followed by the name.  No resources returns ‘No
resources’.  A resource with no text returns ‘resource has no text’.

   The tools ask in ‘ask’ mode and in ‘accept-edits’.  ‘always-approve’
does not ask.  Plan mode does not refuse them.  A read-only subagent may
list and read resources.  A detached chat may do that too.  A failed
call is ‘MCP call failed’.  A command that is not a program is refused
before it starts: ‘MCP command is not a program’.  Nothing is written
into the project.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Computer use,  Next: Monitors,  Prev: MCP,  Up: Tools

10.13 Computer use
==================

Harmless can see the screen, click, type, and open a browser the user
can watch.

   ‘computer_screenshot’ returns the width, the height, and a PNG path
under the Harmless data directory, in ‘computer/’.  The file is not in
the project.

   ‘computer_click’ clicks at ‘x’ and ‘y’.  A missing ‘button’ is left.
Nil and a value that is not an integer are refused: ‘x must be an
integer’ or ‘y must be an integer’.  A negative integer is refused: ‘x
must be a non-negative integer’.  A button other than left, right, or
middle is refused: ‘button must be left, right, or middle’.

   ‘computer_type’ types ‘text’ into the focused window.  Nil and a
value that is not text are refused: ‘text must be a string’.  ‘""’  and
whitespace are refused: ‘text is empty’.  ‘/’ and ‘c:/’ are characters
to type.

   ‘computer_key’ presses one key, such as enter, tab, escape, a
function key from f1 to f12, or one character.  Nil, ‘""’, and
whitespace are refused: ‘key is empty’.  A value that is not text is
refused: ‘key must be a string’.  Anything else, including ‘c:/’, is
refused: ‘key is not a key’.  ‘/’ is a key.

   ‘browser_open’ opens ‘url’ in the desktop browser.  Nil, ‘""’, and
whitespace are refused: ‘url is empty’.  A value that is not text is
refused: ‘url must be a string’.  ‘/’ and ‘c:/’ are refused: ‘url must
be http or https’.

   The class is ‘shell’.  The tools ask in ‘ask’ mode and in
‘accept-edits’.  ‘always-approve’ does not ask.  In plan mode a click,
typed text, a key, and the browser are refused before the question:
‘Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused’.  A screenshot
still runs.  A read-only subagent is not offered these tools.  A
detached chat is offered them.  Nothing is written into the project.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Monitors,  Prev: Computer use,  Up: Tools

10.14 Monitors
==============

‘monitor’ watches one source until a line matches ‘pattern’.  The source
is a ‘path’ inside the project, a ‘command’, or the id of a background
‘shell’.  Pass one of those.  None is refused: ‘monitor source is
missing’.  More than one is refused: ‘monitor source must be one’.

   Nil and a value that is not text are refused: ‘pattern must be a
string’.  ‘""’  and whitespace are refused: ‘pattern is empty’.  A regex
Emacs cannot compile is refused: ‘pattern is not a pattern’.  ‘/’ and
‘c:/’ are patterns, not paths.

   A path is resolved inside the project.  A value that is not text is
refused: ‘path must be a string’.  ‘""’  and whitespace are refused:
‘Path is empty’.  ‘/’, ‘c:/’, and a path that leaves the project are
refused: ‘Path escapes project: ’, followed by the path.  A directory,
including a trailing slash, is refused: ‘monitor path is a directory’.
A missing file is refused: ‘monitor file is missing’.  Lines already in
the file do not match.  Later lines do.

   A command uses the same read-only check as the shell.  A value that
is not text is refused: ‘command must be a string’.  ‘""’  and
whitespace are refused: ‘command is empty’.  A shell id that is not text
is refused: ‘shell id must be a string’.  A negative number is refused:
‘shell id is not an id’.  ‘""’  and whitespace are refused: ‘shell id is
empty’.  An unknown shell id is refused: ‘no background shell ’,
followed by the id.  ‘/’ and ‘c:/’ are ids, not paths.

   A match sends ‘Monitor ’, the id, ‘ matched:’, and the line.  The
session is idle, so that starts a turn.  A session that is already in a
turn queues the line and keeps going.  ‘monitor_stop’ stops the watch.
A command this monitor started is killed.  A background shell that was
only watched keeps running.  Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused:
‘monitor id is empty’.  A value that is not text is refused: ‘monitor id
must be a string’.  A negative number is refused: ‘monitor id is not an
id’.  An unknown id is refused: ‘no monitor ’, followed by the id.
Stopping a monitor that has already stopped is refused: ‘monitor ’, the
id, and ‘ is not running’.

   A file watch does not ask.  A read-only command does not ask.  A
command that can write asks in ‘ask’ mode and in ‘accept-edits’.
‘always-approve’ does not ask.  In plan mode that command is refused:
‘Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can write is refused’.  A
file watch and a read-only command still run.  A read-only subagent may
watch a file or a read-only command.  A detached chat is not offered
these tools.  Nothing is written into the project.  The watch is
forgotten when Emacs exits.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Instructions,  Next: Skills,  Prev: Tools,  Up: Top

11 Instructions
***************

Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

  1. ‘CLAUDE.md’, when ‘harmless-claude-md’ says to read it

  2. ‘AGENTS.md’

  3. ‘HARMLESS.md’

  4. ‘.harmless/HARMLESS.md’

   ‘harmless-claude-md’ has three values.  ‘nil’ never reads
‘CLAUDE.md’.  ‘t’ always reads it.  ‘anthropic’, the default, reads it
only when the session model id starts with ‘claude-’.  A model such as
‘grok-4.6’ does not match.  ‘CLAUDE.md’ is read before the other three
names, so those later files win when they disagree.

   A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty files
are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

   An instruction file can include another file by writing ‘@path’.  The
path is relative to the file that contains the import, not the session
directory.  A backslash before a space is part of the path.  The file's
text replaces the import.  An import inside inline code or a fenced code
block is left as written.  A quoted path is left as written.  The file
must stay inside the session directory.  Harmless refuses an empty path,
a missing file, a directory, a cycle, and a path that leaves the
session.  The filesystem root is not a directory an import may enter.

   The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk ‘default-directory’ looking
for these files.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Skills,  Next: Memory,  Prev: Instructions,  Up: Top

12 Skills
*********

A skill is a directory that contains ‘SKILL.md’.  The file starts with
‘name’ and ‘description’ in YAML frontmatter.  If the name is missing,
Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description is missing, it
uses the first paragraph of the body.

   Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the
path.  The body is not sent until the model reads that file with
‘read_file’.

   Harmless looks for a ‘skills’ directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

‘.agents/skills’
‘.codex/skills’
     Codex.

‘.claude/skills’
     Claude Code.

‘.grok/skills’
     Grok Build.

‘.harmless/skills’
     Harmless.

   In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier
skill of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of
the same name farther away.  ‘/etc/codex/skills’ is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Memory,  Next: Plan mode,  Prev: Skills,  Up: Top

13 Memory
*********

Memory is Markdown under ‘harmless-directory’, in ‘memory’.  Global
notes apply to every project.  Workspace notes belong to one project.  A
detached chat has workspace memory unavailable and global memory
available.

   Each scope has three directories:

‘topics’
     Curated notes, one file per topic.

‘observations/_inbox’
     New facts, not yet folded into a topic.

‘archive’
     Inbox files that ‘harmless-dream’ has folded.

   ‘MEMORY.md’ in each scope is a generated index.  A turn receives the
index, not the notes.

‘M-x harmless-remember’
     Save a title and a note in workspace memory.  A prefix argument
     saves it in global memory.

‘M-x harmless-dream’
     Fold the inbox into topics for this project and for global memory.
     The inbox files move to ‘archive’.

   The model uses three tools.

‘memory_read’
     Read one memory file.  ‘scope’ is ‘workspace’ or ‘global’.

‘memory_remember’
     Append a fact to the inbox.  ‘topic’ is an optional slug, such as
     ‘emacs’.

‘memory_write_topic’
     Replace one topic file with the full Markdown you supply.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Plan mode,  Next: Usage,  Prev: Memory,  Up: Top

14 Plan mode
************

‘M-x harmless-plan’ turns plan mode on for the current session.  A
prefix argument turns it off.  The menu key is ‘P’.  The model may also
call ‘enter_plan_mode’, which asks you before it switches.

   While plan mode is on, the model is told to explore with read-only
tools, to write the plan only with ‘write_plan’, and to call
‘exit_plan_mode’ when the plan is ready.  ‘write_file’, ‘replace’, and
‘apply_patch’ fail.  A shell command that can write is refused before
any permission question: ‘Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that
can write is refused’.  A read-only shell command still runs.
‘shell_kill’ still asks.  A click, typed text, a key, and a browser are
refused: ‘Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused’.  A
screenshot still runs.  *Note Computer use::.  A file watch and a
read-only command still run.  A command that can write is refused.
*Note Monitors::.  The header line shows ‘plan’.  ‘ask_user’ can still
ask a question.  *Note Questions::.

   ‘write_plan’ writes ‘plan.md’ in the session directory.  The contents
replace the whole file.  An empty plan is refused.

   ‘exit_plan_mode’ shows the plan and asks:

     Plan: a approve and build, s request changes, q quit

‘a’
     Turn plan mode off and tell the model to implement the plan.

‘s’
     Ask for revision notes, leave plan mode on, and send those notes
     back.

‘q’
     Turn plan mode off and tell the model to stop without changing
     project files.

   Approving does not apply a patch by itself.  The same turn continues,
and later tool calls do the edits.

   A read-only shell command still runs.  A command that can write is
refused, including when the permission mode is ‘always-approve’.  *Note
Shell::.

   The flag is saved with the session.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Usage,  Next: Files,  Prev: Plan mode,  Up: Top

15 Usage
********

‘M-x harmless-usage’ shows two blocks.

   The session block is the context window of the current model, the
prompt size of the last request, and what remains in the window.  It
then shows the running prompt and completion totals for this chat.
Window sizes that Harmless does not learn from the provider live in
‘harmless-usage-context-windows’.  A model with no known window is
marked unknown.  Before the next prompt, Harmless compacts the
transcript when that prompt size has reached
‘harmless-compact-threshold’ percent of the window.  *Note Transcript::.

   The account block is the last rate-limit remainder each provider
sent.  A connection with no such headers says so.  For the connection
named ‘xAI’, the buffer also asks xAI for the current allowance when you
are not in batch Emacs.  This report is not an account credit balance.

   In the usage buffer, ‘g’ redraws and ‘q’ quits.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Files,  Next: Keys,  Prev: Usage,  Up: Top

16 Files
********

Harmless keeps its own files under ‘harmless-directory’.  The default is
‘harmless/’ in ‘user-emacs-directory’.  Nil and the empty string mean
that same default.  A relative path is refused.  The filesystem root is
refused.  A missing directory is created when it is needed.  It is not
replaced with ‘/’.

‘config.el’
     Optional configuration, loaded once.

‘auth.json’
‘auth-openai.json’
‘auth-anthropic.json’
‘auth-SLUG.json’
     OAuth tokens for each connection.  Mode 0600.

‘sessions/PROJECT/ID/summary.json’
     The session's model, status, totals, and plan flag.

‘sessions/PROJECT/ID/messages.jsonl’
     The transcript, one JSON object per line.  A finding from another
     live session is one of those lines.  *Note Messages::.

‘sessions/PROJECT/ID/plan.md’
     The plan, when plan mode has written one.

‘sessions/_detached/ID/’
     A chat with no project.

‘worktrees/REPOSITORY/NAME/’
     An isolated checkout of that repository.  *Note Worktrees::.

‘memory/global/’
‘memory/workspaces/PROJECT/’
     Notes.  *Note Memory::.

‘computer/’
     Screenshots from ‘computer_screenshot’.  *Note Computer use::.

   PROJECT in a session path is the project directory encoded as one
file name, not the project name you see in the buffer.

   The log is not a file.  It is the buffer ‘*harmless-log*’.  *Note
Trouble::.


